# A header constant's integer value read from clang's front end, nothing run
# (panel 235's R9, its C5, provisional until measured on the four CI legs;
# design.md §4.19, §1.12). A fetched package's unit takes this route since
# defect 2351 (`cli/value_fetched.hero`), since R9 refuses to run a fetched
# header's code at build; a program's own unit keeps panel 216's probe, which
# runs its accessors to judge its constants (`cli/value_ask.hero`).
#
# **The route** (the critic's of panel 235, its finding 11, made strict). The
# unit's opening, as its compile reads its headers, and after it one
# enumerator per constant asked, `enum : long long { hero_constant_<i> =
# (NAME) };`, `unsigned long long` for an unsigned type, compiled
# `-fsyntax-only` under the unit's own words with clang's JSON dump of the
# enumerators alone (`-ast-dump-filter`). An enumerator's initialiser is an
# integer constant expression, so clang's constant evaluator folds it, and
# the dump holds the value of the expression in its own C type, a
# `ConstantExpr`'s `value`, below the conversion to the enumerator's type.
#
# **Strict, or the dump reads what C leaves undefined.** Measured on this
# Mac's Apple clang 21 (2026-11-07, `-std=gnu11`, the unit's dialect), the
# critic's form gave a value at exit 0 with no word for `1 << 40` (the sign
# bit's `-2147483648`), `INT64_MIN / -1` (`INT64_MIN`), `-1 << 3`,
# `(int)1e20` (`2147483647`) and `1 << 31`, every one a value panel 216's
# probe refuses. clang folds such an expression only as a GNU extension,
# once its evaluator has found it is not a constant expression, and says so
# under `-Wgnu-folding-constant`; a shift into the sign bit it says under
# `-Wshift-sign-overflow`. Both are errors here (`STRICT`), and a constant
# whose enumerator clang refuses has no value: it needs the probe, which a
# fetched header may not have. What that refuses beside the undefined: a
# `static const` object and a comma expression, each a value C defines that
# is not an integer constant expression; a float, a string, a record's brace
# list and a call, which no enumerator holds. Each constant is asked twice,
# on two lines, and two values that differ are refused: `__COUNTER__` or
# `__LINE__` gives the accessor's own expansion another one.
#
# **Measured against the probe over the tree, 2026-11-07** (every `constant`
# of `examples/` and `tests/golden/` a unit of its program names, each built
# on this Mac, its value printed by its unit's own opening and words): of 96
# in programs that build, 83 read the value the program reads and 0 another,
# one is a pointer, and 12 are refused, values C defines that need the probe
# (three floats, two comma expressions, two statement expressions,
# `(int)(1000.0 / 60)` and four read through an object or an initialiser),
# and 13 record constants are not asked. **What it does not judge yet**,
# which is why a fetched constant reading a declaration is refused
# (`cli/value_fetched.hero`): a value that reads a declaration, an enumerator
# or an object, is read as that declaration's initialiser made it, and no
# diagnostic on the constant's line sees what C left undefined there. Five
# constants defect 2109's case refuses read here at exit 0 (`enum { EA = 1
# << 31 }` read through `EB = EA + 1`, among them), so each answer names the
# declarations its value reads (`Read.refers`), which a fetched header's
# route refuses.

use cli/clang_tree
use cli/deps
use cli/digest
use cli/process
use cli/publish
use cli/read_back
use cli/response_file
use cli/strings

# One constant asked: its C name and whether the type it is bound as is
# unsigned, which the enumerator's own type follows.
record Asked
    name: str
    unsigned: bool

# One constant's answer: the decimal value clang's evaluator gave the
# expression in its own C type, or why the dump holds none; and the
# declarations its value reads, an enumerator or an object, whose own
# initialiser no diagnostic on the constant's line judges.
record Read
    name: str
    value: str?
    refers: [str]

constant PREFIX: str
    "hero_constant_"

# The second enumerator's suffix.
constant AGAIN: str
    "_again"

# What opens an error's words on a line clang printed.
constant ERROR: str
    ": error: "

# The two warnings that make an enumerator the evaluator could not hold to
# C's rules an error: a GNU fold, and a shift into the sign bit; on the
# command line, and raised again before each enumerator (`text_of`).
constant RAISED: [str]
    [
        "-Wgnu-folding-constant"
        "-Wshift-sign-overflow"
    ]

# On the command line with no limit to the errors told: clang stops telling
# them at its twentieth by default, and a constant past it read its value
# with nothing said (measured on this file's own test of fourteen shapes).
#
# **And C's own meaning of a signed shift, `-fno-wrapv`, on every target**
# (defect 2360, as panel 218's R3 gives the probe the same word): clang 23's
# MSVC target adds `-fwrapv`, under which a shift into the sign bit is
# defined and `-Wshift-sign-overflow` says nothing, so on the Windows box
# `(1 << 31)` read `-2147483648`, and `(1LL << 63)`, `(1i64 << 63)` and
# `((__int64)1 << 63)` read `-9223372036854775808`, each at exit 0 where
# this Mac's clang refuses them (measured 2026-11-08, clang 23.1.1 there,
# Apple clang 21 here, which falls as silent under `-fwrapv`). It is the last
# word, so it wins over one the unit's own words carry, and it changes no
# header's reading, only what C computes.
constant STRICT: [str]
    [
        "-Werror=gnu-folding-constant"
        "-Werror=shift-sign-overflow"
        "-ferror-limit=0"
        "-fno-wrapv"
    ]

# The probe's text: the opening, then each constant asked twice, each time
# the two warnings raised to errors again and its enumerator, so the `i`th
# constant's two enumerators stand on lines `first_line(opening) + 6 * i + 2`
# and `+ 5`. Raised again before each, since a header reaches past its
# region: measured on this Mac, a header closing the region's `push` with a
# `pop` of its own and silencing the two let `INT64_MIN / -1` read at exit 0,
# and a `_Pragma` in one bound value's expansion silenced the next line's.
# Asked twice on two lines, since a value `__COUNTER__` or `__LINE__` moves
# is not the one the accessor's own expansion reads (defect 2145's shape).
function text_of(opening: str, asked: [Asked]) -> str
    out: [str] @= [ended(opening)]
    at: i64 @= 0

    for one in asked
        under: str @= "long long"

        if one.unsigned
            under @ "unsigned long long"

        for suffix in ["", AGAIN]
            for warning in RAISED
                out @ out.push("#pragma clang diagnostic error \"" + warning + "\"\n")

            out @ out.push("enum : " + under + " { " + PREFIX + at.to_str() + suffix + " = (" + one.name + ") };\n")

        at @ at + 1

    return out.join("")

# The text with its last line ended.
function ended(text: str) -> str
    if text.len() == 0 || text[text.len() - 1] == '\n'
        return text
    return text + "\n"

# The line the first enumerator stands on.
function first_line(opening: str) -> i64
    count: i64 @= 1
    text = ended(opening)
    at: i64 @= 0

    while at < text.len()
        if text[at] == '\n'
            count @ count + 1
        at @ at + 1

    return count

# Every constant's answer, asked of clang once under `words`, the unit's
# own, in a file of `build` named by its text; absent where clang did not
# start or wrote no dump.
function values(build: str, words: [str], opening: str, asked: [Asked]) -> [Read]?
    dir = build + "/constant-dump"
    process.mkdir_all(dir)?
    text = text_of(opening, asked: asked)
    unit = dir + "/dump-" + digest.digest(text) + ".c"
    publish.write_whole(path: unit, text: text, keyed: true)?
    out = publish.private_name(dir + "/dump.json")
    err = publish.private_name(dir + "/said.txt")
    head = strings.with_all(list: strings.with_all(list: ["clang"], more: words), more: STRICT)
    line = response_file.carried(line: head, dir: dir)
    ran = process.run(
        program: "clang",
        words: strings.with_all(list: line, more: ["-fsyntax-only", "-Xclang", "-ast-dump=json", "-Xclang", "-ast-dump-filter=" + PREFIX, unit]),
        out: out,
        err: err
    )
    dumped = read_back.words(out)
    said = read_back.words(err).default("")
    publish.discard(out)
    publish.discard(err)

    if !ran.started || dumped.is_err()
        return fail(code: "unasked", msg: "clang wrote no dump of the constants' enumerators")
    tree = clang_tree.read(dumped.must())?
    return ok(answers(tree, said: said, unit: unit, first: first_line(opening), asked: asked))

# Each asked constant's value from the dump, or clang's first error on its
# enumerator's line, or that the dump holds no value for it; with the
# declarations its value reads.
function answers(tree: clang_tree.Tree, said: str, unit: str, first: i64, asked: [Asked]) -> [Read]
    found: {str: str} @= {}
    reads: {str: [str]} @= {}
    at: i64 @= 0

    for node in tree.nodes
        if node.kind == "EnumConstantDecl" && strings.starts_with(text: node.name, prefix: PREFIX)
            value = folded(tree, at: at)

            if value.len() > 0
                found[node.name] @ value
            reads[node.name] @ referred(tree, at: at)
        at @ at + 1

    refused = errors(said: said, unit: unit, first: first, count: asked.len())
    out: [Read] @= []
    none: [str] = []
    index: i64 @= 0

    for one in asked
        why = refused[index]
        value = found[PREFIX + index.to_str()]
        again = found[PREFIX + index.to_str() + AGAIN]
        refers = reads[PREFIX + index.to_str()].default(none)

        if why.len() > 0
            out @ out.push(Read(name: one.name, value: fail(code: "refused", msg: why), refers: refers))
        else if !value.is_err() && (again.is_err() || again.must() != value.must())
            out @ out.push(
                Read(name: one.name, value: fail(code: "moving", msg: "its value moves with where it is expanded, so the dump's is not the one its accessor reads"), refers: refers)
            )
        else if value.is_err()
            out @ out.push(
                Read(name: one.name, value: fail(code: "unread", msg: "the dump holds no value for its enumerator"), refers: refers)
            )
        else
            out @ out.push(Read(name: one.name, value: ok(value.must()), refers: refers))
        index @ index + 1

    return out

# The value of the `ConstantExpr` an enumerator's initialiser is, below the
# conversions to the enumerator's type, `""` where there is none.
function folded(tree: clang_tree.Tree, at: i64) -> str
    node: i64 @= at

    while tree.children[node].len() > 0
        node @ tree.children[node][0]

        if tree.nodes[node].kind == "ConstantExpr"
            return tree.nodes[node].value

        if tree.nodes[node].kind != "ImplicitCastExpr"
            return ""

    return ""

# The names of the declarations below node `at` that a reference names, each
# once, in the order the dump writes them.
function referred(tree: clang_tree.Tree, at: i64) -> [str]
    out: [str] @= []
    seen: {str: bool} @= {}
    open: [i64] @= [at]

    while open.len() > 0
        node = open[open.len() - 1]
        rest: [i64] @= []
        k: i64 @= 0

        while k + 1 < open.len()
            rest @ rest.push(open[k])
            k @ k + 1

        open @ rest
        one = tree.nodes[node]

        if one.ref_name.len() > 0 && seen[one.ref_name].is_err()
            seen[one.ref_name] @ true
            out @ out.push(one.ref_name)
        back: i64 @= tree.children[node].len() - 1

        while back >= 0
            open @ open.push(tree.children[node][back])
            back @ back - 1

    return out

# clang's first error on each constant's two lines, `""` where it said none;
# the `i`th constant's stand on lines `first + 6 * i + 2` and `+ 5`
# (`text_of`).
function errors(said: str, unit: str, first: i64, count: i64) -> [str]
    out: [str] @= []

    for _ in range(from: 0, to: count)
        out @ out.push("")

    prefix = unit + ":"

    for line in deps.lines(said)
        if !strings.starts_with(text: line, prefix: prefix)
            continue
        rest = line.slice(from: prefix.len(), to: line.len())
        number = leading_number(rest)
        at = found_at(text: rest, part: ERROR)
        index = (number - first - 2) / 6
        place = (number - first - 2) % 6

        if number < first + 2 || place != 0 && place != 3 || index >= count || at < 0
            continue

        if out[index].len() == 0
            out[index] @ rest.slice(from: at + ERROR.len(), to: rest.len())

    return out

# Where `part` first stands in `text`, or -1.
function found_at(text: str, part: str) -> i64
    at: i64 @= 0

    while at + part.len() <= text.len()
        if text.slice(from: at, to: at + part.len()) == part
            return at
        at @ at + 1

    return -1

# The number a text opens with, or -1.
function leading_number(text: str) -> i64
    value: i64 @= 0
    at: i64 @= 0

    while at < text.len() && text[at] >= '0' && text[at] <= '9' && at < 9
        value @ value * 10 + to_i64(text[at] - '0').must()
        at @ at + 1

    if at == 0
        return -1
    return value

## Tests

# The words the tests ask clang under: the unit's dialect, which the shapes'
# verdicts are measured in.
constant DIALECT: [str]
    ["-std=gnu11"]

# **An integer, a `sizeof`, an `enum` member, a shifted expression and a
# macro to a literal are read from the dump, and nothing runs**: the header
# holds a constructor that would write a file, and none is left behind. The
# member is the one answer that reads a declaration, and says so.
test "a header constant's integer value is read from clang's dump with nothing run (panel 235's C5)"
    dir = "build/selftest-constant-dump-read"
    assert !process.remove_tree(dir).is_err()
    assert !process.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    ran = dir + "/RAN.txt"
    opening = "#include <stdint.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\nenum hero_colour { HERO_RED = 7, HERO_GREEN };\n#define HERO_LIT 42\n#define HERO_TO_LIT HERO_LIT\n#define HERO_SZ sizeof(int32_t[3])\n#define HERO_BIG ((1LL << 40) + 3)\n#define HERO_NEG (-5)\n#define HERO_TOP UINT64_MAX\n__attribute__((constructor)) static void hero_ran(void) { FILE *f = fopen(\"" + ran + "\", \"a\"); if (f) { fputs(\"ran\\n\", f); fclose(f); } }\n"
    asked = [
        Asked(name: "HERO_LIT", unsigned: false)
        Asked(name: "HERO_SZ", unsigned: true)
        Asked(name: "HERO_GREEN", unsigned: false)
        Asked(name: "HERO_BIG", unsigned: false)
        Asked(name: "HERO_TO_LIT", unsigned: false)
        Asked(name: "HERO_NEG", unsigned: false)
        Asked(name: "HERO_TOP", unsigned: true)
    ]
    got = values(build: dir, words: DIALECT, opening: opening, asked: asked).must()
    want = ["42", "12", "8", "1099511627779", "42", "-5", "18446744073709551615"]
    at: i64 @= 0

    for one in got
        assert one.name == asked[at].name
        assert one.value.must() == want[at]
        at @ at + 1

    assert got[2].refers == ["HERO_GREEN"]
    assert got[0].refers.len() == 0
    assert got[1].refers.len() == 0
    assert read_file(ran).is_err()
    assert !process.remove_tree(dir).is_err()

# **What C leaves undefined, what is no integer constant expression, what is
# no integer and what moves with its expansion have no value from the dump**:
# each was read at exit 0 by the critic's form, or is no integer at all, and
# each is refused here. A header that closes its region and silences the two
# warnings, and a `_Pragma` inside a value, are measured shapes the raising
# before each enumerator holds against (`text_of`). And a shift into the sign
# bit of an `int` or a `long long` under the words of a target that defines
# it, `-fwrapv`, clang 23's MSVC target's (defect 2360).
test "a value C leaves undefined, one that is no integer constant expression or no integer, and one that moves have no value from the dump (panel 235's C5)"
    dir = "build/selftest-constant-dump-refused"
    assert !process.remove_tree(dir).is_err()
    assert !process.mkdir_all(dir).is_err()
    hostile = "#pragma clang diagnostic push\n#pragma clang diagnostic pop\n#pragma clang diagnostic pop\n#pragma clang diagnostic ignored \"-Wgnu-folding-constant\"\n#pragma clang diagnostic ignored \"-Wshift-sign-overflow\"\n#pragma clang diagnostic push\n"
    opening = "#include <stdint.h>\n#include <limits.h>\n" + hostile + "static const int hero_k = 5;\nint hero_f(void);\n#define HERO_MINDIV (INT64_MIN / -1)\n#define HERO_SIGN (1 << 31)\n#define HERO_PAST (1 << 40)\n#define HERO_CAST ((int)1e20)\n#define HERO_OVER (INT_MAX + 1)\n#define HERO_SILENCED (_Pragma(\"clang diagnostic ignored \\\"-Wgnu-folding-constant\\\"\") INT64_MIN / -1)\n#define HERO_AFTER (INT64_MIN / -1)\n#define HERO_FLOAT 1.5\n#define HERO_STR \"abc\"\n#define HERO_CALL hero_f()\n#define HERO_OBJECT hero_k\n#define HERO_COMMA (1, 2)\n#define HERO_COUNTED (__COUNTER__ + 1)\n#define HERO_LINE __LINE__\n#define HERO_LL63 (1LL << 63)\n"
    names = [
        "HERO_MINDIV"
        "HERO_SIGN"
        "HERO_PAST"
        "HERO_CAST"
        "HERO_OVER"
        "HERO_SILENCED"
        "HERO_AFTER"
        "HERO_FLOAT"
        "HERO_STR"
        "HERO_CALL"
        "HERO_OBJECT"
        "HERO_COMMA"
        "HERO_COUNTED"
        "HERO_LINE"
        "HERO_LL63"
    ]
    asked: [Asked] @= []

    for name in names
        asked @ asked.push(Asked(name: name, unsigned: false))

    # Under the unit's words, and under words carrying `-fwrapv`, which clang
    # 23's MSVC target adds and under which a shift into the sign bit read a
    # value on the Windows box (defect 2360): this Mac's clang falls as silent
    # given it, so the second ask is the one that fails here too without the
    # reading's own `-fno-wrapv`. Each constant that has a value is named.
    for words in [DIALECT, strings.with_all(list: DIALECT, more: ["-fwrapv"])]
        got = values(build: dir, words: words, opening: opening, asked: asked).must()
        assert got.len() == names.len()
        valued: [str] @= []

        for one in got
            if !one.value.is_err()
                valued @ valued.push(one.name)

        assert valued.join(" ") == ""

    assert !process.remove_tree(dir).is_err()

# **An error is the constant's whose line it stands on**: two lines a
# constant's two enumerators, the second three below the first, the three
# lines past the opening's the first constant's.
test "clang's errors are each the constant's whose enumerator line they name"
    said = "/b/d.c:7:38: error: first\n/b/d.c:7:40: error: second on the line\n/b/d.c:13:1: error: third\n/b/h.h:2:1: error: in the header\n/b/d.c:4:1: warning: not an error\n"
    assert errors(said: said, unit: "/b/d.c", first: 5, count: 3) == ["first", "third", ""]
    assert first_line("a\nb\n") == 3
    assert first_line("a\nb") == 3
    assert text_of("x\n", asked: [Asked(name: "K", unsigned: true)]) == "x\n#pragma clang diagnostic error \"-Wgnu-folding-constant\"\n#pragma clang diagnostic error \"-Wshift-sign-overflow\"\nenum : unsigned long long { hero_constant_0 = (K) };\n#pragma clang diagnostic error \"-Wgnu-folding-constant\"\n#pragma clang diagnostic error \"-Wshift-sign-overflow\"\nenum : unsigned long long { hero_constant_0_again = (K) };\n"
